  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "👑 Game of Thrones Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names from every corner of Westeros, with a house of your own or the right bastard surname."
    },
    "intro": "George R. R. Martin builds most Westerosi names from real <a href=\"medieval-name-generator.html\">medieval</a> English ones with a letter changed: Eddard for Edward, Jaime for James, Petyr for Peter. Each region has its own flavour, Targaryens sound Valyrian, and bastards get a surname that tells you where they were born. This **Game of Thrones name generator** builds names for eight regions of Westeros, plus Valyrian and Free Folk names, as a noble with a new house, a bastard or a first name alone.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Bastard surnames by region",
        "content": "<p>In Westeros, a highborn bastard can't use their father's name. They take a surname from the region they were born in, which is why Jon is a Snow and Ellaria is a Sand:</p><ul><li><strong>Snow:</strong> the North</li><li><strong>Sand:</strong> Dorne</li><li><strong>Rivers:</strong> the Riverlands</li><li><strong>Stone:</strong> the Vale</li><li><strong>Hill:</strong> the Westerlands</li><li><strong>Flowers:</strong> the Reach</li><li><strong>Storm:</strong> the Stormlands</li><li><strong>Pyke:</strong> the Iron Islands</li><li><strong>Waters:</strong> the Crownlands, around King's Landing</li></ul><p>The generator's <em>Bastard</em> format gives each region its right surname. Valyrian names get Waters, as a Targaryen bastard born near King's Landing would.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How each region sounds",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>The North:</strong> plain and old: Rickard, Benjen, Lyanna. Northern houses sound cold and hard.</li><li><strong>The Westerlands:</strong> polished and proud, the Lannisters' country: Tywin, Kevan, Cersei.</li><li><strong>The Reach:</strong> courtly and flowery, home of the Tyrells: Mace, Loras, Margaery.</li><li><strong>Dorne:</strong> a different flavour altogether, after the Rhoynar who settled there: Oberyn, Doran, Arianne, Nymeria.</li><li><strong>The Iron Islands:</strong> harsh and salty: Balon, Euron, Asha.</li><li><strong>Valyrian:</strong> the Targaryens' names, full of ae and y: Aegon, Rhaegar, Daenerys, Viserys.</li><li><strong>The Free Folk:</strong> one name, often with an <a href=\"epithet-generator.html\">epithet</a> earned beyond the Wall: Tormund Giantsbane.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Making your character's name",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Take a real name and change a letter.</strong> It's Martin's own trick: Walter becomes Walder, Robert stays Robert, Edward becomes Eddard.</li><li><strong>Choose a house with a sigil in mind.</strong> A house name like Frostmark or Saltspear suggests its own banner.</li><li><strong>Decide if they're legitimate.</strong> A bastard's surname follows them everywhere, and changes the story.</li></ul><p>All the first names and houses here are new, chosen so they don't belong to a major character. For their sword, try the <a href=\"game-of-thrones-sword-name-generator.html\">Game of Thrones Sword Name Generator</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What are the bastard surnames in Game of Thrones?",
        "answer": "Each region has one: Snow (the North), Sand (Dorne), Rivers (Riverlands), Stone (Vale), Hill (Westerlands), Flowers (Reach), Storm (Stormlands), Pyke (Iron Islands) and Waters (Crownlands)."
      },
      {
        "question": "How does George R. R. Martin come up with names?",
        "answer": "Many are real medieval English names with a letter changed or a spelling twisted: Eddard (Edward), Jaime (James), Petyr (Peter), Walder (Walter)."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are these names from the books or the show?",
        "answer": "The bastard surnames are; the first names and houses are new, so your character won't belong to a family in the books."
      }
    ]
  }
